From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Peter Lemenkov <lemenkov@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 30 Mar 2026 10:27:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix install.sh: use basename when installing LIBSBCL objects

The loop that installs linkable runtime objects reads the LIBSBCL
variable from src/runtime/sbcl.mk and uses each value verbatim as both
the cp source and destination path:

    for i in $(grep '^LIBSBCL=' src/runtime/sbcl.mk | cut -d= -f2-) ; do
        cp "src/runtime/$i" "$BUILD_ROOT$SBCL_HOME/$i"
    done

On most architectures LIBSBCL expands to a plain filename (e.g.
sbcl.o), so this works fine. However on ppc64le, LIBSBCL includes
../../tlsf-bsd/tlsf/tlsf.o, because that object is linked directly into
the runtime on that platform. The relative path traversal is valid for
the source side (relative to src/runtime/), but when used verbatim as
the destination it resolves to a path outside the install tree
($SBCL_HOME/../../tlsf-bsd/tlsf/tlsf.o), which does not exist, causing
install.sh to abort with:

    cp: cannot create regular file '.../lib/sbcl/../../tlsf-bsd/tlsf/tlsf.o':
    No such file or directory

Fix by using $(basename $i) for the destination, so all LIBSBCL objects
are installed flat into $SBCL_HOME regardless of where they were found
in the build tree.

Note: this bug is only triggered when --with-sb-linkable-runtime is
enabled, since without it no LIBSBCL= line is written to sbcl.mk and the
loop never executes.

Signed-off-by: Peter Lemenkov <lemenkov@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: Claude (Anthropic) <https://claude.ai>

diff --git a/install.sh b/install.sh
index 52bd2048c..eadda3628 100755
--- a/install.sh
+++ b/install.sh
@@ -118,7 +118,7 @@ test -f src/runtime/libsbcl.so && \
 
 cp src/runtime/sbcl.mk "$BUILD_ROOT$SBCL_HOME"/sbcl.mk
 for i in $(grep '^LIBSBCL=' src/runtime/sbcl.mk | cut -d= -f2-) ; do
-    cp "src/runtime/$i" "$BUILD_ROOT$SBCL_HOME/$i"
+    cp "src/runtime/$i" "$BUILD_ROOT$SBCL_HOME/$(basename $i)"
 done
 
 # installing contrib
